nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">Hybrid inheritance is a mix of two or more types of inheritance. However, Java does not support hybrid inheritance directly to avoid complexity and ambiguity.</p><p id="a5aa" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">Multiple Inheritance (through Interfaces)</p><p id="47ce" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">Java does not support multiple inheritance directly due to the â€œdiamond problem.â€ However, it can be achieved using interfaces.</p><figure class="oj ok ol om on oo og oh paragraph-image"><div role="button" tabindex="0" class="op oq fz or bi os"><span class="ge gf gg ao gh gi gj fs gk speechify-ignore">Press enter or click to view image in full size</span><div class="og oh oi"><picture><source srcset="https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:640/format:webp/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 640w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:720/format:webp/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 720w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:750/format:webp/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 750w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:786/format:webp/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 786w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:828/format:webp/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 828w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1100/format:webp/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 1100w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/format:webp/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 1400w" sizes="(min-resolution: 4dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 50vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 4) and (max-width: 700px) 50vw, (min-resolution: 3dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 67vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 3) and (max-width: 700px) 65vw, (min-resolution: 2.5dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 80vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 2.5) and (max-width: 700px) 80vw, (min-resolution: 2dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 100vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 2) and (max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" type="image/webp"><source data-testid="og" srcset="https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:640/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 640w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:720/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 720w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:750/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 750w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:786/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 786w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:828/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 828w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1100/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 1100w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/0*LU4XfdlU9GS9jpNF 1400w" sizes="(min-resolution: 4dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 50vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 4) and (max-width: 700px) 50vw, (min-resolution: 3dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 67vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 3) and (max-width: 700px) 65vw, (min-resolution: 2.5dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 80vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 2.5) and (max-width: 700px) 80vw, (min-resolution: 2dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 100vw, (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 2) and (max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px"><img alt="" class="bi mh ot c" width="700" height="704" loading="lazy" role="presentation"></picture></div></div></figure><p id="2ae4" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Advantages of Inheritance</strong></p><p id="6ed4" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Code Reusability:</strong> Inheritance promotes the reuse of existing code, reducing redundancy and improving efficiency.</p><p id="3d14" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Method Overriding:</strong> Inheritance allows for method overriding, where a child class can provide a specific implementation for a method already defined in its parent class.</p><p id="8a96" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Improved Code Maintenance:</strong> By centralizing common features in a parent class, inheritance makes it easier to manage and update the code.</p><p id="53e5" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Disadvantages of Inheritance</strong></p><p id="73fc" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Increased Complexity:</strong> Inheritance can introduce complexity, especially in deep inheritance hierarchies, making the code harder to understand and maintain.</p><p id="045b" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Tight Coupling:</strong> Inheritance creates a tight coupling between the parent and child classes, which can limit flexibility and hinder changes.</p><p id="c897" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Polymorphism and Inheritance</strong></p><p id="7f28" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">Polymorphism allows objects to be treated as instances of their parent class rather than their actual class. This enables a single interface to be used for a general class of actions.</p><p id="71e4" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">How Inheritance Supports Polymorphism</strong></p><p id="72ad" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">Inheritance enables polymorphism by allowing a subclass to be treated as an instance of its superclass, facilitating method overriding and the use of generic code.</p><p id="deba" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl"><strong class="nc ht">Method Overriding:</strong> Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides a specific implementation for a method that is already defined in its superclass.</p><p id="4f7d" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">Rules for Method Overriding</p><p id="86b0" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">The method must have the same name, return type, and parameters as the method in the parent class.</p><p id="830d" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">The method must be marked as public or protected (not private).</p><p id="5366" class="pw-post-body-paragraph na nb hs nc b nd ne nf ng nh ni nj nk nl nm nn no np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx hl bl">The @Override annotation is used to indicate that a method is being overridden.</p><figure class="oj ok ol om on oo og oh paragraph-image"><div role="button" tabindex="0" class="op oq fz or bi os"><span class="ge gf gg ao gh gi gj fs gk speechify-ignore">Press enter or click to view image in full size</span><div class="og oh ow"><picture><source srcset="https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:640/format:webp/0*A7MxW7VNZrzc6uOs 640w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:720/format:webp/0*A7MxW7VNZrzc6uOs 720w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:750/format:webp/0*A7MxW7VNZrzc6uOs 750w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:786/format:webp/0*A7MxW7VNZrzc6uOs 786w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:828/format:webp/0*A7MxW7VNZrzc6uOs 828w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1100/format:webp/0*A7MxW7VNZrzc6uOs 1100w, https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/format:webp/0*A7MxW7VNZrzc6uOs 1400w" sizes="(min-resolution: 4dppx) and (max-width: 700px) 50vw, (-webkit-min-dev